invariant: INV-50
file: sha512.c
catches: hkdf384_test_host
reason: after a host object's sha512_update compresses an input's whole
  blocks where the caller holds them, it copies the bytes left from the
  input's start and not from past those blocks. An update of less than a
  block, or of whole blocks alone, is right, so the short published
  vectors pass. An update of a block and more hashes its first bytes a
  second time in place of its last. bin/hkdf384_test_host runs RFC
  4231's HMAC-SHA-384 cases on that path, among them the two with a
  131-byte key, which HMAC hashes in one call, and the one with a
  152-byte message, and they fail.

--- old
        compress_blocks(s->h, in, whole);
        in += whole * SHA512_BLOCK;
        n -= whole * SHA512_BLOCK;
--- new
        compress_blocks(s->h, in, whole);
        n -= whole * SHA512_BLOCK;
